      teen: 'If you have ever been told (or felt) that you are "too young to matter yet" -- Scripture disagrees, and it names ages to prove it. Joseph: seventeen (Genesis 37:2). David facing Goliath: "but a youth" (1 Samuel 17:33). Josiah seeking God: sixteen (2 Chronicles 34:3). Jeremiah: sent as "a child" (Jeremiah 1:7). Here is the science that makes this urgent, not just encouraging: in the teen brain the reward-and-emotion system runs HOT while the judgment-center is still finishing (into the mid-20s). That is why you can KNOW something is unwise and still feel a massive pull to do it -- that gap is partly physical and God-designed. So God’s answer is not "wait until 25." It is TRAIN NOW: "train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old, he will not depart from it" (Proverbs 22:6). What you pour in now -- the Word, real friendships, worship, wise mentors -- is literally shaping the adult you will be. "Let no man despise thy youth" (1 Timothy 4:12) -- and do not despise it yourself.',
      senior: 'For the seasoned believer and the parent or grandparent, this lesson is a charge: God calls the young, and their forming is not a reason to wait but the reason to invest. He named the ages -- Joseph seventeen (Genesis 37:2), thirty at his rise (Genesis 41:46); David "but a youth" (1 Samuel 17:33); Josiah seeking God at sixteen (2 Chronicles 34:3); Jeremiah "a child" (Jeremiah 1:6-7); Timothy taught the Scriptures "from a child" (2 Timothy 3:15). And the design confirms the urgency: the adolescent brain’s reward and emotional circuitry runs strong while the judgment-center completes last -- so the years when the young most FEEL the pull of folly are the very years their frame is most being SET. That is why the command is "train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old, he will not depart from it" (Proverbs 22:6). Do not merely forbid the young; FORM them -- repeat the Word, model the walk, be patient with the gap between their knowing and their doing, and pour in knowledge of God before the enemy pours in his strategy (the next lesson). You have the frame they are still building; hand it forward.',
