    // Word as source. Every verse KJV-verbatim (DR-0076).
    title: 'Converting Power Into Knowledge — Fruitful in Old Age',
    anchor: { ref: 'Job 12:12; Psalm 92:14; Colossians 2:3', theme: 'With the ancient is wisdom; and in length of days understanding. They shall still bring forth fruit in old age. In Christ are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge. Raw quickness fades; deep knowledge compounds -- so you can grow MORE fruitful with age, not less.' },
    bigIdea: 'The world worships youth and mental quickness -- and then panics as it fades. Philip Ackerman’s PPIK theory reframes the whole thing: raw processing power (working memory, reasoning speed) does peak in late adolescence and slowly decline -- but that speed is only STARTING CAPITAL. Real adult intelligence is what you CONVERT it into: deep, durable, transferable KNOWLEDGE. Ackerman names four parts -- PROCESS (the raw ability), PERSONALITY (the habit of actually engaging your mind, choosing demanding tasks), INTERESTS (the steering wheel that focuses your effort), and KNOWLEDGE (the goal). The remarkable result: by converting processing into deep domain mastery, a person can become MORE effective with age -- the seasoned surgeon out-performs the quick novice not by speed but by depth. Scripture said this long ago and made it a promise: "With the ancient is wisdom; and in length of days understanding" (Job 12:12); "The hoary head is a crown of glory, if it be found in the way of righteousness" (Proverbs 16:31); and of the righteous, "They shall still bring forth FRUIT IN OLD AGE; they shall be fat and flourishing" (Psalm 92:14). So the call is not to cling to quickness but to invest it -- "grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ" (2 Peter 3:18) -- because the deepest knowledge of all is a Person, "in whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge" (Colossians 2:3).',
    inApp: 'This week, invest your quickness instead of hoarding it. FIRST, ENGAGE ON PURPOSE: pick one cognitively demanding thing (a hard passage, a real skill, a deep book) and lean in -- the habit of choosing demanding work is what turns raw ability into knowledge (Proverbs 4:7, "with all thy getting get understanding"). SECOND, AIM YOUR INTERESTS: your curiosity is a steering wheel -- point it at what compounds, above all at knowing God, "grow in grace, and in the knowledge" (2 Peter 3:18). THIRD, BUILD FOR THE LONG GAME: you are converting today’s energy into knowledge you will still be fruitful with at eighty -- "they shall still bring forth fruit in old age" (Psalm 92:14). And keep the guardrail: knowledge is for love, not for pride -- "knowledge puffeth up, but charity edifieth" (1 Corinthians 8:1); the point is depth that serves people and honours God, not cleverness that impresses them. If you are young, start converting now; if you are older, you are not declining -- you are compounding.',
    benefits: [
      'A reframe of aging: raw quickness fades, but deep KNOWLEDGE compounds -- "with the ancient is wisdom; and in length of days understanding" (Job 12:12).',
      'A promise for the long game: "they shall still bring forth fruit in old age; they shall be fat and flourishing" (Psalm 92:14) -- more fruitful, not less.',
      'The habit that converts ability to knowledge: engaging demanding work on purpose -- "with all thy getting get understanding" (Proverbs 4:7); "give instruction to a wise man, and he will be yet wiser" (Proverbs 9:9).',
      'The deepest knowledge is a Person: "in whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge" (Colossians 2:3); "grow in grace, and in the knowledge" (2 Peter 3:18).',
      'The guardrail: knowledge serves love, not pride -- "knowledge puffeth up, but charity edifieth" (1 Corinthians 8:1).',
    ],
    levels: {
      child: 'Some people think being SMART is all about being FAST -- answering quickly. But here is a secret grown-ups often forget: the best kind of smart is DEEP knowing, and that grows BIGGER the older you get! The Bible says older people have wisdom "with the ancient is wisdom" (Job 12:12), and that people who love God "shall still bring forth fruit in old age" (Psalm 92:14) -- like a tree that keeps making fruit even when it is very old! So do not just try to be quick. Learn things DEEPLY, especially about God, and keep learning your whole life. And remember: being smart is for HELPING and LOVING people, not for showing off (1 Corinthians 8:1). Learn deep, love big.',
      teen: 'Everyone in school worships mental SPEED -- who is quickest, who tests fastest. Philip Ackerman’s research (PPIK) says that is only the starting line. Yes, raw processing speed peaks young and slowly declines -- but that speed is just CAPITAL to invest. What actually makes someone impressive over a lifetime is converting that raw ability into deep, durable KNOWLEDGE: real mastery of a field. That is why a seasoned expert beats a quick beginner -- not by speed, but by depth. Four things drive it: your raw ability, your HABIT of engaging hard things, your INTERESTS (which focus your effort), and the KNOWLEDGE you build. Here is the freeing part: you are not racing a clock that is running out; you are compounding something that grows for life -- "they shall still bring forth fruit in old age" (Psalm 92:14); "with the ancient is wisdom" (Job 12:12). So stop chasing "quick" and start building DEEP -- especially in knowing God, "grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord" (2 Peter 3:18), because "in [Christ] are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge" (Colossians 2:3). And keep it aimed at love, not showing off (1 Corinthians 8:1).',
      senior: 'For the seasoned believer, this lesson is quiet vindication and clear commission. The culture’s anxiety about fading "quickness" misunderstands the design. Ackerman’s PPIK theory holds that raw processing speed peaks young and declines, but that it is merely capital to be converted, over a lifetime, into deep, transferable KNOWLEDGE -- so that mastery can rise even as speed falls, and the seasoned practitioner outperforms the quick novice by depth. Scripture treated this as normal and even glorious: "With the ancient is wisdom; and in length of days understanding" (Job 12:12); "The hoary head is a crown of glory, if it be found in the way of righteousness" (Proverbs 16:31); and the flourishing image of the righteous -- "planted in the house of the LORD... they shall still bring forth fruit in old age; they shall be fat and flourishing" (Psalm 92:13-14). Your decades are not decline; they are compounded knowledge, and above all compounded KNOWING of God -- the goal Daniel names, "the people that do know their God shall be strong, and do exploits." So do two things. Keep converting -- "grow in grace, and in the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ" (2 Peter 3:18); a wise man instructed grows "yet wiser" (Proverbs 9:9). And hand it forward: the young have the speed and lack the depth; you have the depth. Pour the treasure out -- keeping it always in its place, for "knowledge puffeth up, but charity edifieth" (1 Corinthians 8:1), and the summit of all knowing is the One "in whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge" (Colossians 2:3).',
    },
