    lesson: 'This lesson is for anyone who has carried a wound and quietly wondered, "What is wrong with me?" -- and the first thing to hear is that the question itself may be aimed wrong. A psychiatrist who works with trauma puts it plainly: the brain is not BROKEN; it is WIRED TO SURVIVE. When something overwhelming happens, the brain does precisely what its Maker built it to do -- it records the sensory details of the danger and lays down protective networks so that, next time, it can react fast enough to keep you alive. The trouble is that this good survival system can get miscalibrated by real pain, so the alarm keeps firing when the danger is long past: hypervigilance, a startle you cannot reason away, panic that rises from nowhere, avoidance, and other "survival-mode" behaviors. Here is the pivotal insight, and it is a mercy: those reactions are BIOLOGICAL, not moral failings. They are not proof that you are weak, faithless, or bad. They are a protective system doing its job imperfectly -- and simply understanding them that way tends to move a person out of the pit of self-blame and toward real help and real healing. Now let the Word give this its full weight, because Scripture honours the wounded far more tenderly than shame ever will. First, DIGNITY: even the survivor-wiring is part of a frame God calls wonderful -- "I will praise thee; for I am fearfully and wonderfully made" (Psalm 139:14). Second, NO CONDEMNATION: the misfiring alarm is not sin to be scolded -- "There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus" (Romans 8:1). Third, and this is the one that re-roots a person, IDENTITY: you are not your trauma. It was something that happened TO you; it is not the name of who you are. "Therefore if any man be in Christ, he is a NEW CREATURE: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new" (2 Corinthians 5:17). Fourth, a real HEALER who specializes in exactly this wound: "He healeth the broken in heart, and bindeth up their wounds" (Psalm 147:3); "The LORD is nigh unto them that are of a broken heart; and saveth such as be of a contrite spirit" (Psalm 34:18); the Messiah announced His mission as "to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound" (Isaiah 61:1). There is a practical, faith-filled way to work WITH the design. When the alarm fires, rather than adding shame to fear, you can almost thank the system -- "my brain is trying to protect me" -- and then, in prayer, ask God to help you re-teach it what no longer needs guarding; this is the renewed mind of lesson four applied to a wounded place, casting the care on Him "for he careth for you" (1 Peter 5:7) and receiving "the peace of God, which passeth all understanding" that "shall keep your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus" (Philippians 4:7). And like Joseph, a person can eventually come to read even the evil done to them under God’s redeeming hand -- "ye thought evil against me; but God meant it unto good" (Genesis 50:20) -- while remembering that suffering is not always punishment (John 9:3). Now two boundaries, kept on purpose. First, on the SCIENCE: this house holds the specific clinical claims lightly -- brain-scan "patterns," or the idea that spiritual capacity lives in a particular lobe of the brain, are that clinician’s own interpretations, interesting but not established, and not doctrine here; the Spirit’s work in a person is not reducible to a scan. We take the well-supported, humane core -- the brain is wired to survive, the responses are biological, identity is separable from the wound -- and we let the Word, not the scan, be the authority on the soul. Second, and most important, on CARE: this lesson is reflection WITH the Healer through His Word. It is NOT therapy, and it does not diagnose anyone. Deep wounds are meant to be carried in company, not alone; bringing a trusted pastor, a mature believer, or a competent professional alongside you is not a lack of faith -- it is the wisdom God built into the Body, where we "bear ye one another’s burdens." If you are deeply wounded or unsafe, reach for real help now. The God who "bindeth up their wounds" often does His binding through the hands of the people He puts beside you. You were wired to survive; in Christ you are made new; and the Healer is near. (On the timeline: this is the Church-Age ministry of the One anointed "to bind up the brokenhearted," carried by His Body; it pairs with the Living Lessons "The God Who Documents His Grief," "A Sound Mind," and "Fearfully and Wonderfully Made.")',
